      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(160px,1fr));gap:8px;margin-bottom:10px;">
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Fraction of large galaxies</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">~60–70%</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Example</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">Milky Way, Andromeda, M51 (Whirlpool)</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Characteristic</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">Active star formation in arms</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="muted-copy">Spiral galaxies have a central bulge (older, redder stars), a flat disc with spiral arms (younger, bluer stars, gas clouds, active star formation), and a spherical halo of older stars and dark matter. Barred spirals (like the Milky Way) have a central bar structure from which the arms emerge. The spiral pattern is not fixed structure -- it is a density wave that rotates at a different rate to the stars themselves, sweeping through the disc and triggering star formation as it compresses gas clouds.</div>`
    },
    {
      tab: 'Elliptical',
      content: `<div class="u-fs-14px u-fw-500 text-primary u-mb-md">Elliptical galaxies -- the aged giants</div>
