        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Fraction of large galaxies</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">~20% (but dominate by mass)</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Example</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">M87 (hosts famous BH), Virgo A</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Characteristic</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">Little gas, minimal star formation</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="muted-copy">Elliptical galaxies contain mostly old, red stars with very little gas or dust and almost no new star formation -- they have "used up" their raw material. They range from nearly spherical (E0) to very elongated (E7). The largest known galaxies are giant ellipticals, formed by mergers of multiple spirals. When the Milky Way and Andromeda collide in ~4.5 billion years, they will likely merge into an elliptical galaxy over billions of years -- astronomers call the future result "Milkomeda."</div>`
    },
    {
      tab: 'Irregular',
      content: `<div class="u-fs-14px u-fw-500 text-primary u-mb-md">Irregular galaxies -- chaos and star-forming intensity</div>
      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(160px,1fr));gap:8px;margin-bottom:10px;">
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Fraction of galaxies</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">~20–25% (mostly small)</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Example</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">Large Magellanic Cloud, Small MC</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Characteristic</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">No defined structure; intense star birth</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="muted-copy">Irregular galaxies lack the organised structure of spirals or ellipticals. They are typically smaller, gas-rich, and forming stars at a furious rate. Many are the result of gravitational interactions with larger neighbours -- the Large and Small Magellanic Clouds (visible from the Southern Hemisphere) are dwarf irregular galaxies being tidally disrupted by the Milky Way. Dwarf galaxies of all types are the most numerous in the universe; most large galaxies have dozens of satellite dwarfs orbiting them.</div>`
    },
    {
      tab: 'Active galaxies',
      content: `<div class="u-fs-14px u-fw-500 text-primary u-mb-md">Active galactic nuclei -- when the central black hole is feeding</div>
      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(160px,1fr));gap:8px;margin-bottom:10px;">
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Types</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">Seyfert, Quasar, Blazar, Radio galaxy</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Energy source</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">Accretion onto supermassive BH</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Quasar brightness</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">Up to 10,000× entire galaxy</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="muted-copy">When a supermassive black hole actively accretes material, the infall releases enormous energy -- often outshining the entire galaxy of stars. Quasars (quasi-stellar objects) are the most luminous sustained energy sources in the universe, visible across billions of light-years. A blazar is a quasar whose jet happens to point directly at us, making it even brighter. These objects are all found in the early universe -- our galaxy's own central black hole (Sgr A*) is currently "quiet," but shows evidence of past activity.</div>`
