        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Exoplanets found</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">~1,000+</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Best for</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">Massive planets in tight orbits</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">First exoplanet discovery</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">51 Pegasi b (1995, Nobel Prize 2019)</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="muted-copy">A planet does not orbit its star -- both the star and the planet orbit their common centre of mass. This causes the star to "wobble" slightly. As the star moves toward or away from us, its light is Doppler-shifted (blueshift / redshift) in proportion to its velocity. This is how Mayor and Queloz discovered 51 Pegasi b in 1995 -- a "hot Jupiter" orbiting closer to its star than Mercury does the Sun. The discovery overturned planetary formation models: such giant planets were thought impossible at those distances. Method is biased toward massive planets and tight orbits -- the induced stellar wobble for an Earth-like planet at 1 AU is only ~0.1 m/s.</div>`
    },
    {
      tab: 'Direct imaging',
      content: `<div class="u-fs-14px u-fw-500 text-primary u-mb-md">Direct imaging -- actually photographing the planet</div>
      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(160px,1fr));gap:8px;margin-bottom:10px;">
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Exoplanets found</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">~60 (enormously difficult)</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Best for</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">Young, massive planets far from bright stars</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Key tool</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">James Webb Space Telescope (JWST)</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="muted-copy">The challenge: a star is typically 10 billion times brighter than the reflected light from a planet. Direct imaging requires blocking the star's light (coronagraph) while preserving the planet's. Only young, massive planets at large orbital distances have been imaged -- they are warm enough to emit their own infrared radiation. HR 8799 hosts four directly imaged planets, all Jupiter-mass or larger. JWST's coronagraph capability has expanded direct imaging possibilities dramatically. Future missions (like the Habitable Worlds Observatory planned for the 2040s) aim to directly image Earth-like planets and analyse their atmospheres for biosignatures.</div>`
    },
    {
      tab: 'Habitable zone',
      content: `<div class="u-fs-14px u-fw-500 text-primary u-mb-md">Habitable zone -- the "Goldilocks" distance</div>
      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(160px,1fr));gap:8px;margin-bottom:10px;">
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">HZ candidates found</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">50+ potentially habitable planets</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Best candidate</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">TRAPPIST-1e, f (39 light-years)</div></div>
        <div class="sub-card-compact"><div class="muted-text-small">Habitability caveat</div><div class="u-fs-12px u-fw-500 text-primary">Liquid water is necessary but not sufficient</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="muted-copy">The habitable zone is the range of orbital distances where liquid water could exist on a planet's surface -- given the star's luminosity. The TRAPPIST-1 system (a red dwarf 39 light-years away) hosts 7 rocky planets, three of which are in the habitable zone. JWST has begun analysing their atmospheres -- TRAPPIST-1c appears to lack a thick CO₂ atmosphere, hinting at a Venus-like fate. The habitable zone is a crude concept: Europa (Jupiter's moon) has a liquid water ocean under an ice shell despite being far from the Sun -- heated by tidal forces. Life may exist in more places than simple orbital distance predicts.</div>`
    }
  ];

  buildGenericTabs('exo-tabs', 'exo-panel', exoMethods);

  /* ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     WORMHOLE TYPE TABS
  ───────────────────────────────────────────── */
  const whTypes = [
    {
      tab: 'Schwarzschild',
      content: `<div class="u-fs-14px u-fw-500 text-primary u-mb-md">Schwarzschild wormhole (Einstein-Rosen bridge, 1935)</div>
      <div class="muted-copy">The original wormhole -- discovered by Einstein and Rosen as a mathematical curiosity in the Schwarzschild solution to general relativity. It connects two separate exterior regions of spacetime through the interior of a black hole. Crucially, it is non-traversable: the throat collapses faster than the speed of light, so nothing can pass through. It is more of a mathematical feature than a physical tunnel. If you tried to enter, you would reach the singularity long before you could exit the other side.</div>`
    },
    {
      tab: 'Morris-Thorne',
      content: `<div class="u-fs-14px u-fw-500 text-primary u-mb-md">Morris-Thorne traversable wormhole (1988)</div>
      <div class="muted-copy">The first mathematically rigorous design for a traversable wormhole, published by Kip Thorne and Mike Morris in response to a question from Carl Sagan (who was writing Contact). It requires exotic matter with negative energy density to hold the throat open. Thorne showed that general relativity permits such a construction in principle, but the exotic matter requirement is a near-insurmountable obstacle. The paper opened a serious scientific discussion about wormholes and, unexpectedly, led to the investigation of time travel (closed timelike curves) as a genuine physics topic.</div>`
    },
    {
      tab: 'Kerr wormhole',
      content: `<div class="u-fs-14px u-fw-500 text-primary u-mb-md">Kerr wormhole -- inside a rotating black hole</div>
      <div class="muted-copy">The mathematics of a rotating (Kerr) black hole allows for a ring-shaped singularity instead of a point. In principle, a trajectory through the ring could avoid the singularity and emerge in a different region of spacetime -- effectively a wormhole. However, the inner horizon of a Kerr black hole is believed to be dynamically unstable (mass inflation instability): any perturbation, even a single photon, causes energy to diverge to infinity. Most physicists believe Kerr wormholes are non-traversable in any realistic scenario despite the appealing mathematics.</div>`
    },
    {
      tab: 'ER = EPR',
      content: `<div class="u-fs-14px u-fw-500 text-primary u-mb-md">ER = EPR -- wormholes and quantum entanglement (2013)</div>
      <div class="muted-copy">In a landmark 2013 paper, Juan Maldacena and Leonard Susskind proposed that quantum entanglement (Einstein-Podolsky-Rosen pairs, or EPR) and wormholes (Einstein-Rosen bridges, ER) may be the same phenomenon described in different languages. Two entangled black holes are connected by a non-traversable wormhole. This conjecture -- ER = EPR -- has become one of the most discussed ideas in theoretical physics, offering a possible bridge between quantum mechanics and general relativity. It does not allow faster-than-light communication (entanglement never does), but it suggests spacetime geometry may be built from quantum information. Experimentally probing this link is a major goal of quantum gravity research.</div>`
    }
  ];

  buildGenericTabs('wh-tabs', 'wh-panel', whTypes);

  /* ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     BLACK HOLE ANATOMY INTERACTIVE
  ───────────────────────────────────────────── */
  const bhDetails = {
    outer: {
      title: 'Accretion disc and relativistic jets',
      text: 'Gas and dust spiralling toward a black hole form an accretion disc -- heated to millions of degrees by friction, emitting X-rays. This is how we detect black holes: not the black hole itself, but the glowing disc around it. Along the rotation axis, powerful jets of plasma are launched at near-light speed, extending for thousands of light-years. The mechanism (likely magnetic fields anchored in the disc) is not fully understood. M87\'s jet extends 5,000 light-years and was the first jet ever imaged, by Heber Curtis in 1918.'
    },
    accr: {
      title: 'Inner accretion disc -- the hottest region',
      text: 'As material spirals inward, it moves faster and faster, heating to ~100 million degrees close to the event horizon. This region emits most of the disc\'s energy. Turbulent magnetic fields thread the disc, extracting angular momentum and allowing material to fall inward. Time dilation from the black hole\'s gravity means clocks in this region run significantly slower than those far away -- a clock at the ISCO of a stellar black hole would run ~15% slower than one at infinity.'
    },
    isco: {
      title: 'ISCO -- innermost stable circular orbit',
      text: 'The ISCO is the closest orbit where a particle can stably circle a black hole without spiralling in. For a non-rotating (Schwarzschild) black hole it is at 3 Schwarzschild radii. For a maximally rotating (Kerr) black hole it can be as close as 0.5 r_s (co-rotating) or as far as 4.5 r_s (counter-rotating). Matter crossing the ISCO loses the ability to maintain a stable orbit and plunges into the black hole in less than a millisecond. The position of the ISCO is observable from the X-ray spectrum of the accretion disc and allows astronomers to measure a black hole\'s spin.'
    },
    photo: {
      title: 'Photon sphere -- light in permanent orbit',
      text: 'At 1.5 Schwarzschild radii, photons can theoretically orbit the black hole in circular paths. An observer at the photon sphere would be able to see their own back. However, these orbits are unstable -- any perturbation sends photons either inward to the event horizon or outward to escape. The photon sphere is what creates the dark "shadow" seen in the 2019 Event Horizon Telescope image of M87*: the bright ring is photons from the accretion disc bent around the photon sphere, while the dark region inside is the shadow -- not the event horizon itself, but the region from which light cannot escape our view.'
    },
    eh: {
      title: 'Event horizon -- the point of no return',
      text: 'The event horizon is not a physical surface -- there is no wall, no sudden change in local physics. A freely falling observer would pass through it without noticing (for a large enough black hole, tidal forces at the horizon are survivable). The event horizon is a mathematical surface: the boundary from which the escape velocity equals the speed of light. Once crossed, every possible future trajectory leads to the singularity. For a stellar black hole the crossing takes milliseconds of proper time. General relativity predicts that from a distant observer\'s perspective, in-falling matter appears to asymptotically approach the horizon, freezing and redshifting to invisibility -- but never actually crossing. The in-falling observer disagrees.'
    },
    ergo: {
      title: 'Ergosphere -- frame dragging and the Penrose process',
      text: 'Rotating black holes (Kerr black holes) drag spacetime itself along with their rotation -- "frame dragging." Outside the event horizon but within a region called the ergosphere, spacetime is dragged so fast that nothing can remain stationary; everything must co-rotate with the black hole. However, the ergosphere is still outside the event horizon, so escape is possible. The Penrose process (1971) shows that energy can be extracted from the ergosphere: a particle splitting in two, with one fragment falling into the black hole and the other escaping with more energy than the original particle had. This is the mechanism behind quasar jets and suggests rotating black holes are vast energy reservoirs.'
    },
    sing: {
      title: 'Singularity -- where physics breaks down',
      text: 'At the centre (r = 0 for Schwarzschild, a ring for Kerr), general relativity predicts a singularity: infinite density, infinite curvature. This is almost universally regarded as a sign that general relativity is incomplete -- not that a physical infinity actually exists. Quantum gravity (the not-yet-achieved unification of quantum mechanics and general relativity) is expected to resolve singularities, replacing them with something finite. The Penrose-Hawking singularity theorems (1965-1970) proved that singularities are inevitable inside event horizons given reasonable physical assumptions -- Penrose won the 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics for this work.'
    }
  };

  document.querySelectorAll('[data-bh]').forEach(el => {
    el.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const key = el.getAttribute('data-bh');
      const detail = bhDetails[key];
      if (!detail) return;
      document.getElementById('bh-detail-title').textContent = detail.title;
      document.getElementById('bh-detail-text').textContent = detail.text;
      document.querySelectorAll('[data-bh]').forEach(e => e.style.outline = 'none');
      el.style.outline = '2px solid rgba(192,128,255,0.7)';
    });
  });

  /* ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     INITIAL RENDERS
  ───────────────────────────────────────────── */
  showPlanet('earth');
  updateDist();
  
</script>
